We are given that the rectangular coordinates of the point are $(10,3),$ and the polar coordinates are $(r,\theta).$ Therefore, substituting $x = 10$ and $y = 3$ into the conversion equations and solving for $r$ and $\theta,$ we have: \begin{align*} 10 &= r (\cos \theta) \\ 3 &= r (\sin \theta) \end{align*}
Squaring and adding the two equations, we find $r = \sqrt{10^2 + 3^2} = \sqrt{109}.$  Dividing the second equation by the first, we have $\tan \theta = \frac{3}{10},$ so $\theta = \tan^{-1} \frac{3}{10}.$
To convert from $(r,\theta) = (\sqrt{109},\tan^{-1} \frac{3}{10})$ to rectangular coordinates $(x', y'),$ plug into the conversion equations: \begin{align*} x' &= r (\cos \theta) = \sqrt{109} \left( \cos \left( \tan^{-1} \frac{3}{10} \right) \right) \\ y' &= r (\sin \theta) = \sqrt{109} \left( \sin \left( \tan^{-1} \frac{3}{10} \right) \right) \end{align*}
